WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.524
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.824 --> 00:00:09.118
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.418 --> 00:00:14.891
Unit six: Bioethics. Today you will debate medical ethics using modal necessity.

4
00:00:15.191 --> 00:00:17.890
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:18.833 --> 00:00:22.136
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:22.983 --> 00:00:24.449
Informed consent.

7
00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:30.561
Informed consent means a patient's voluntary agreement to treatment after understanding the risks.

8
00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:34.403
Informed consent was obtained before the operation began.

9
00:00:35.017 --> 00:00:38.342
Your turn. Say: Informed consent.

10
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:42.631
Informed consent.

11
00:00:43.383 --> 00:00:44.733
Genetic engineering.

12
00:00:45.717 --> 00:00:50.003
Genetic engineering means deliberately changing the DNA of an organism.

13
00:00:50.617 --> 00:00:54.810
Genetic engineering could cure inherited diseases, but it raises fears.

14
00:00:55.417 --> 00:00:58.394
Your turn. Say: Genetic engineering.

15
00:01:01.233 --> 00:01:02.629
Genetic engineering.

16
00:01:03.383 --> 00:01:04.675
Bodily autonomy.

17
00:01:05.683 --> 00:01:09.819
Bodily autonomy means a person's right to govern what happens to their own body.

18
00:01:10.417 --> 00:01:13.612
Bodily autonomy underpins the right to refuse treatment.

19
00:01:14.217 --> 00:01:17.345
Your turn. Say: Bodily autonomy.

20
00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:22.130
Bodily autonomy.

21
00:01:22.883 --> 00:01:24.082
Beneficence.

22
00:01:25.083 --> 00:01:28.476
Beneficence means the duty to act for the benefit of others.

23
00:01:29.083 --> 00:01:32.604
Beneficence obliges doctors to promote their patients' wellbeing.

24
00:01:33.217 --> 00:01:36.275
Your turn. Say: Beneficence.

25
00:01:39.133 --> 00:01:40.227
Beneficence.

26
00:01:40.983 --> 00:01:42.472
Non-maleficence.

27
00:01:43.483 --> 00:01:46.934
Non-maleficence means the duty to avoid causing harm.

28
00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:51.628
Non-maleficence is often summarised as 'first, do no harm'.

29
00:01:52.217 --> 00:01:55.345
Your turn. Say: Non-maleficence.

30
00:01:58.200 --> 00:01:59.828
Non-maleficence.

31
00:02:00.583 --> 00:02:02.235
Distributive justice.

32
00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:07.664
Distributive justice means the fair allocation of resources and benefits.

33
00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:12.177
Distributive justice asks who should receive scarce organs first.

34
00:02:12.783 --> 00:02:16.016
Your turn. Say: Distributive justice.

35
00:02:18.867 --> 00:02:20.390
Distributive justice.

36
00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:22.453
Moral status.

37
00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:27.806
Moral status means the degree to which a being's interests deserve consideration.

38
00:02:28.417 --> 00:02:31.902
Debate continues over the moral status of an early embryo.

39
00:02:32.517 --> 00:02:35.355
Your turn. Say: Moral status.

40
00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:39.619
Moral status.

41
00:02:40.383 --> 00:02:41.675
Ethical dilemma.

42
00:02:42.683 --> 00:02:47.133
Ethical dilemma means a situation with two conflicting moral obligations.

43
00:02:47.717 --> 00:02:51.562
Whether to disclose a risky diagnosis posed an ethical dilemma.

44
00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:55.197
Your turn. Say: Ethical dilemma.

45
00:02:58.033 --> 00:02:59.464
Ethical dilemma.

46
00:03:00.217 --> 00:03:01.218
Personhood.

47
00:03:02.217 --> 00:03:06.132
Personhood means the status of being a person with full moral rights.

48
00:03:06.717 --> 00:03:09.738
Personhood is central to debates about the beginning of life.

49
00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:12.759
Your turn. Say: Personhood.

50
00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:16.717
Personhood.

51
00:03:17.483 --> 00:03:18.868
Regulation gap.

52
00:03:19.883 --> 00:03:23.799
Regulation gap means an area where technology has outpaced the law.

53
00:03:24.383 --> 00:03:28.589
A regulation gap left gene-editing clinics largely unsupervised.

54
00:03:29.183 --> 00:03:32.590
Your turn. Say: Regulation gap.

55
00:03:35.433 --> 00:03:36.818
Regulation gap.

56
00:03:37.633 --> 00:03:41.017
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:41.883 --> 00:03:47.183
Visal, a clinic offered to edit my cousin's embryo to prevent a disease. Should that be allowed?

58
00:03:48.283 --> 00:03:57.170
That is a profound ethical dilemma. Informed consent must be obtained before any procedure, but an embryo cannot give it.

59
00:03:58.283 --> 00:03:59.749
Then the parents decide?

60
00:04:00.850 --> 00:04:11.513
Perhaps, but they need not have unlimited authority. Procedures that alter future generations must be justified by serious medical need, not preference.

61
00:04:12.617 --> 00:04:14.407
And who sets those limits?

62
00:04:15.517 --> 00:04:24.926
Regulators must. Where a regulation gap exists, patients may be exploited, so oversight cannot be left to individual clinics.

63
00:04:26.067 --> 00:04:27.579
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:28.417 --> 00:04:31.717
Grammar focus: Modal necessity in ethics.

65
00:04:32.017 --> 00:04:33.192
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:33.883 --> 00:04:37.160
Informed consent must be obtained before any procedure.

67
00:04:38.050 --> 00:04:41.002
Doctors ought to respect their patients' bodily autonomy.

68
00:04:41.917 --> 00:04:44.462
Regulators should close the regulation gap.

69
00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:54.313
Moral necessity: must (strongest), ought to / should (strong advice). No 'to' after must and should. Use 'need not' to say something is not required.

70
00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:59.037
Be careful! Don't say: Consent must to be obtained first.

71
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:04.091
Say: Consent must be obtained first.

72
00:05:04.391 --> 00:05:06.193
No 'to' after a modal verb.

73
00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:09.838
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:05:10.633 --> 00:05:12.087
The Limits of Medicine.

75
00:05:12.387 --> 00:05:19.994
Modern biotechnology allows interventions that earlier generations could scarcely have imagined, from gene editing to organ transplantation.

76
00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:29.675
Bioethics offers principles for evaluating them: respect for autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence and distributive justice.

77
00:05:30.667 --> 00:05:39.528
Informed consent must be obtained, yet it cannot be given by embryos, children or those who lack capacity, which produces enduring ethical dilemmas.

78
00:05:40.533 --> 00:05:47.038
Questions of moral status and personhood divide thoughtful people, so that regulators must balance competing convictions.

79
00:05:48.033 --> 00:05:56.128
Because technology advances faster than legislation, a regulation gap may leave patients vulnerable unless the law is updated promptly.

80
00:05:57.233 --> 00:06:01.328
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:06:02.183 --> 00:06:05.112
Informed consent blank be obtained before surgery.

82
00:06:05.412 --> 00:06:08.728
A, must. B, must to. C, musts.

83
00:06:12.267 --> 00:06:13.918
The answer is A: must.

84
00:06:15.083 --> 00:06:17.652
The duty to avoid causing harm is blank.

85
00:06:17.952 --> 00:06:22.604
A, beneficence. B, non-maleficence. C, personhood.

86
00:06:26.167 --> 00:06:29.130
The answer is B: non-maleficence.

87
00:06:30.283 --> 00:06:33.792
The fair allocation of scarce resources is blank justice.

88
00:06:34.092 --> 00:06:37.920
A, distributive. B, bodily. C, informed.

89
00:06:41.467 --> 00:06:43.815
The answer is A: distributive.

90
00:06:44.950 --> 00:06:46.961
Which means 'it is not required'?

91
00:06:47.261 --> 00:06:51.809
A, You must not attend.. B, You need not attend.. C, You cannot attend..

92
00:06:55.367 --> 00:06:57.982
The answer is B: You need not attend..

93
00:06:59.117 --> 00:07:02.103
According to the reading, a regulation gap occurs when…

94
00:07:02.403 --> 00:07:08.669
A, laws are too strict. B, technology outpaces legislation. C, patients refuse treatment.

95
00:07:12.233 --> 00:07:15.789
The answer is B: technology outpaces legislation.

96
00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:20.024
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:07:20.883 --> 00:07:24.323
What ethical limits, if any, should apply to genetic engineering?

98
00:07:41.117 --> 00:07:42.605
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:42.905 --> 00:08:18.573
I believe genetic engineering should be permitted to prevent serious disease, but it must be strictly regulated. Informed consent is difficult when future generations are affected, so decisions need independent oversight. Enhancement for appearance or advantage is more troubling, because it threatens distributive justice: only the wealthy could afford it. Regulators ought to act before the technology spreads. Limits are not obstacles to progress; they are what make progress trustworthy.

100
00:08:18.873 --> 00:08:20.605
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:08:21.517 --> 00:08:25.771
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and modal necessity in ethics.

102
00:08:26.071 --> 00:08:30.593
Today's value is moral courage. Do what is right even when it is difficult.

103
00:08:30.893 --> 00:08:33.290
See you in Unit seven. Goodbye!

104
00:08:33.590 --> 00:08:36.962
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

